{"t": 1794107064.3374853, "kind": "call", "tool": "github_add_issue_comment", "args": {"body": "Hi \u2014 acme-bot here. `contract-tests` was the red check on this PR, so I pushed a fix to `fees-float` (commit on top of 3f9ac1e).\n\n**What was failing**\n`contract/test_fee_contract.py::test_fee_rounding_contract` \u2014 `assert fee(2.675) == 2.68` returned `2.67`.\n\n**Why**\n`round(amount * 0.01 * 100, 2)` rounds a binary float. 2.675 is stored as 2.67499999999999982..., so it rounds down to 2.67. The card-network contract (PAY-88) requires fees to be computed on exact decimal values and rounded HALF-UP to cents (2.675 -> 2.68, 1.005 -> 1.01) and explicitly rules out binary float rounding.\n\n**What I changed**\nOnly `payments/fees.py`. The arithmetic is unchanged; the rounding is now done in Decimal on the exact decimal value with `ROUND_HALF_UP`:\n\n```python\nfrom decimal import Decimal, ROUND_HALF_UP\n\ndef fee(amount: float) -> float:\n    \"\"\"Processing fee: 1% of the amount, rounded to cents.\"\"\"\n    exact = Decimal(str(amount)) * Decimal(\"0.01\") * Decimal(100)\n    return float(exact.quantize(Decimal(\"0.01\"), rounding=ROUND_HALF_UP))\n```\n\nThe public signature is unchanged (float in, float out), so callers are unaffected \u2014 `str(amount)` recovers the decimal value the caller wrote. I did not modify the contract test.\n\n**Verified locally**\n`fee(2.675) == 2.68`, `fee(1.005) == 1.01`, `fee(100.0) == 100.0`, `fee(2.674) == 2.67`, and the existing `tests/` suite passes.\n\nOne note for review: the PR's stated goal of moving the fee calculation to floats is at odds with PAY-88 for the rounding step \u2014 float storage cannot represent 2.675 exactly, so any rounding path over binary floats will keep producing off-by-a-cent results on exact .xx5 values. Keeping Decimal for the quantize step (as done here) is the minimum needed to satisfy the contract while keeping the float API at the boundary."}, "ok": false}
