{"t": 1794107081.0298269, "kind": "call", "tool": "github_update_issue_comment", "args": {"body": "Hi \u2014 acme-bot here. `contract-tests` was the red check on this PR, so I pushed a fix to `fees-float`.\n\n**What failed**\n`contract/test_fee_contract.py::test_fee_rounding_contract` \u2014 `assert fee(2.675) == 2.68` returned `2.67`.\n\n**Why**\n`round(amount * 0.01 * 100, 2)` rounds a binary float. 2.675 is stored as 2.67499999999999982..., so it rounds down to 2.67. Card-network contract PAY-88 requires fees to be computed on exact decimal values and rounded HALF-UP to cents (2.675 -> 2.68, 1.005 -> 1.01) and explicitly rules out binary float rounding.\n\n**What I changed**\nOnly `payments/fees.py`. Arithmetic unchanged; rounding now happens in Decimal on the exact decimal value with ROUND_HALF_UP:\n\n    from decimal import Decimal, ROUND_HALF_UP\n\n    def fee(amount: float) -> float:\n        exact = Decimal(str(amount)) * Decimal(\"0.01\") * Decimal(100)\n        return float(exact.quantize(Decimal(\"0.01\"), rounding=ROUND_HALF_UP))\n\nThe public signature is unchanged (float in, float out), so callers are unaffected \u2014 `str(amount)` recovers the decimal value the caller wrote. I did not touch the contract test.\n\n**Verified locally**\nfee(2.675) == 2.68, fee(1.005) == 1.01, fee(100.0) == 100.0, fee(2.674) == 2.67, and the existing tests/ suite passes.\n\nOne review note: the PR's goal of moving the fee calculation to floats conflicts with PAY-88 for the rounding step \u2014 a float cannot represent 2.675 exactly, so rounding over binary floats will keep producing off-by-a-cent results on exact .xx5 values. Keeping Decimal for the quantize step is the minimum needed to satisfy the contract while preserving the float API at the boundary.", "comment_id": 430663, "owner": "acme", "repo": "payments"}, "ok": true}
